    "crud-learn-ops": {
      "id": "crud-learn-ops",
      "resources": [
        {
          "label": "MDN: HTTP request methods",
          "url": "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Methods"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Learn: the four things every stored list goes through",
      "kind": "lesson",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "data-crud"
      ],
      "objectives": [
        "Name the four CRUD operations and the HTTP verb that carries each one.",
        "Trace create, read, update and delete on a task list and say how the list changes after each.",
        "Explain why each operation names a record by id rather than by its position in the list."
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Almost every program that stores something does four things to it, over and over: create a new record, read one back, update it, and delete it. Those four words are the whole of CRUD. You meet them here on the task list you already know, and then again in the data and behavior phases as if they needed no introduction. This is where they get that introduction."
        },
        {
          "kind": "example",
          "body": "The four moves, each one run on a list of tasks:",
          "code": "create  add a task              the list grows by one\nread    get the task with id 2   nothing changes\nupdate  mark that task done      the same task, changed\ndelete  remove that task         the list shrinks by one"
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "Two habits make these four safe to build on. First, a good operation hands back a NEW list instead of editing the one it was given, so a change you decide to undo never half happened. Second, each operation names the record it targets by an id, a value that marks one item out from all the rest, because a position shifts the moment anything is added."
        },
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "The same four have a face on the web from the last phase: POST creates, GET reads, PUT or PATCH updates, DELETE removes. When a server finishes a create, it answers 201, a success that means there is one more thing than before. Learn CRUD on the list and those HTTP verbs stop being a second thing to memorize. They are the same four moves with network names."
        },
        {
          "kind": "keypoints",
          "body": "Key Points",
          "items": [
            "Create makes a record; read, update, delete each act on one.",
            "Operations name a record by id; a position is not a stable name.",
            "A good operation returns a new list and leaves the old untouched.",
            "HTTP verbs are CRUD over a network: POST, GET, PATCH, DELETE."
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "task",
          "body": "Before any code, write the four operations as you would do them to a to-do list: add a task, look one up, mark one finished, remove one. For each, say which record it targets and what the list looks like when it is done. Then name the HTTP verb that carries each one from the last phase. If all eight line up, you hold the whole idea of this phase."
        }
      ],
      "visualization": {
        "type": "state-compare",
        "spec": {
          "before": {
            "title": "Before: two tasks on the list",
            "lines": [
              "Buy milk",
              "Write report"
            ]
          },
          "after": {
            "title": "After create: one task added",
            "lines": [
              "Buy milk",
              "Write report",
              "Call the bank"
            ]
          }
        }
      },
      "hints": [
        "Read each operation as a before and an after: the list it starts with, and the one list it hands back changed.",
        "Create and delete change how many records there are. Read and update leave the count alone and touch only one."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "Updating means replacing the whole list. An update changes one record and returns a list that still holds every other one.",
        "A task is found by its position in the list. Positions move as soon as a task is added or removed, so only the id names one record reliably.",
        "CRUD is a database word with nothing to do with the web. It is the very same four moves that POST, GET, PUT, and DELETE carry across a network."
      ]
    },
    "crud-ask-verb": {
      "id": "crud-ask-verb",
      "title": "Reason: which of the four is this action",
      "kind": "question",
      "mode": "learn",
      "guidance": 1,
      "competencyIds": [
        "data-crud"
      ],
      "lesson": [
        {
          "kind": "prose",
          "body": "You tap the control that marks a task finished, and the app calls the server so the change sticks. Which of the four operations is this, and which HTTP verb carries it? Decide the answer and the reason before you read the explanation."
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        "Update, sent as PUT or PATCH, because one task that already exists is being changed.",
        "Delete, sent as DELETE, because marking a task done clears it off the list.",
        "Create, sent as POST, because a finished state is something new the server has never seen.",
        "Read, sent as GET, because the app is only checking whether the task was already done."
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explanation": "Marking a task done adds nothing and takes nothing away, so it is neither create nor delete, and it is not a read, because the app is telling the server something rather than asking. One existing record is being changed, and that is exactly what an update does; over the network it arrives as a PUT or a PATCH carrying the new value. The task keeps its id the whole time, which is the tell. Update is the only one of the four that leaves both the count and the identity of the records alone while editing what one of them holds.",
      "hints": [
        "Ask which of the four leaves the number of tasks unchanged and edits one record that already has an id. That is the update."
      ],
      "misconceptions": [
        "A PATCH that sends data must be a create. It sends a change to a record the id already names, so nothing new comes into being.",
        "Marking done adds a second, finished copy of the task. It changes the one task already there and keeps its id."
      ]
    },
